              "background": "#F0F0F0",
              "text_color": "#1a1a1a",
              "content": {
                "en": "<h2>Copper uglies vs purging: are they the same?</h2><p>&ldquo;Purging&rdquo; is a word borrowed from exfoliating products. People use it for a rough patch they believe is skin clearing itself before it improves.</p><p>No study has shown that this happens with copper peptides, and we do not use either word as an explanation. If your skin looks worse, the sensible assumption is that it may be reacting to something, not that it is part of a process you should wait out.</p><h2>What causes copper uglies: what is known and what is guesswork</h2><p><strong>Known:</strong> a small share of people react to almost any skin product, and one woman in the trial did.</p><p><strong>Guesswork:</strong> everything else. You will read that copper peptides first &ldquo;break down&rdquo; skin before building it back up. That idea comes from laboratory work on cells. It has not been measured in faces, and we do not present it as fact. Other guesses, such as the amount of copper or the strength of the formula, are untested too.</p>"
              }
            }
          }
        },
        "block_order": ["m"],
        "custom_css": [
